// `#[...]` data attributes attach to a callable's PARAMETERS, on a free function and on a method
// alike. They land in the shared manifest like any other attribute, keyed `callable#param` — a
// distinct `#` separator rather than a third dotted component, because `build.target` would read
// equally as "parameter `target` of fn `build`" and "method `target` of type `build`", and every
// consumer that splits a target would have had to guess. The `#` makes the key self-describing.
//
// This is what per-argument metadata exists for: `#[Arg(help: …)]` describes exactly one parameter,
// and riding on the fn-level attribute as a parallel positional list would silently mean something
// else the first time someone reordered the signature. Built statically, so both backends agree.
// expect: stdout "build#target -> target triple"
// expect: stdout "build#release -> optimize"
// expect: stdout "Tools.run#cmd -> the command"
// expect: exit 0
@attribute(Param)
struct Arg { help: string }

fn build(#[Arg(help: "target triple")] target: string, #[Arg(help: "optimize")] release: bool): void {
    return;
}

class Tools {
    fn new(): Tools { return Tools {} }
    fn run(#[Arg(help: "the command")] cmd: string): string { return cmd }
}

// Several parameters of one callable each carry their own attribute, and each surfaces separately
// under its own key — the property a parallel side-list cannot offer.
for a in attributes_of::<Arg>() {
    echo "${a.target} -> ${a.value.help}";
}
